derived from.

These files quote text from Wikipedia and Wikisource, or are derived from the
Tajik Shahnameh corpus (ArabovMK/shahnameh-tajik-corpus), and are licensed under
CC BY-SA 4.0 instead (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/):
ezafe-written-dev.jsonl, ezafe-junctions-dev.jsonl, ezafe-lab-1.jsonl,
arabic-spans-lab.jsonl, arabic-lines-lab.jsonl, shahnameh-tajik-test.jsonl.

arabic-model.json.gz is CC BY-SA 4.0: word and character statistics and model
parameters, no source passages and no copy of the Quran. Built from arwikisource,
arwikibooks, Aya, Ganjoor, fawikisource, Dehkhoda, books and fawiki; Quran
wording found with Tanzil's text marked where Arabic is quoted; calibrated on
lines from fawikisource, Naskban, books, Ganjoor, fawiki, fawikiquote, Tasnim,
IRIB News, Mizan, HPLT, Virgool, Telegram channels. Its provenance field lists
each source's recorded licence.

corpus-sample.jsonl.gz is the Sample tier of the Ghazal Corpus 1.0.0, 500
documents, each under the licence in its own `license` field: CC BY-SA 4.0
(Wikimedia text; its authors are in the history of each document's url), CC0 1.0
(Common Voice) or public domain (Ganjoor poems whose poets died long ago).
corpus-sample.json is its manifest, with the full attribution statement.

verb-stems.tsv and verb-preverbs.tsv include lexical facts checked against
English Wiktionary (Wiktionary contributors, via the Kaikki Persian extract)
and are distributed under CC BY-SA 4.0:
https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/
https://en.wiktionary.org/
https://kaikki.org/dictionary/Persian/
Dehkhoda infinitive entries were also consulted for stem facts; no dictionary
definitions or corpus passages are reproduced in these tables.

orthography-splits.tsv and orthography-copula.tsv are licensed under CC BY-SA 4.0
instead of the default above. Both contain word forms and
counts from rows of the Ghazal Corpus ingestion (2026-11-09),
not passages. Sources: Persian Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0), Mizan parallel
corpus (CC BY 4.0 dataset), IRIB news, Blogfa, Ganjoor, the books collection
and persian-tweets-2024. The latter five have no licence recorded in the
ingestion; their text is not redistributed. No Tanzil or Sokhan dictionary
material is used. Count selection and thresholds are in each file's header.
orthography-splits.tsv uses the package's verb-stems.tsv, verb-preverbs.tsv
and verb-colloquial.tsv for its finite-verb gate; their inherited sources
include standard paradigms and John Mace's Persian Grammar, Appendix I.
The corpus behind the legacy preverb counts is unspecified.

orthography-function-splits.tsv, orthography-content-splits.tsv,
and orthography-he-endings.tsv are also licensed
under CC BY-SA 4.0 instead of the default. All three use counts from fawiki,
mizan, irib-news, blogfa, books, persian-tweets-2024 and ganjoor. The first
two also use common-word/name evidence from the training splits of UD
Persian Seraji and PerDT (CC BY-SA 4.0 datasets). orthography-he-endings.tsv
and orthography-copula.tsv use the existing orthography-silent-he.tsv as
a candidate gate; that legacy file records 2026 corpus spelling counts
without a complete source list. Each file's header identifies its inputs.

orthography-mi-forms.tsv and orthography-clitic-hosts.tsv are licensed under
CC BY-SA 4.0. Both use word-form counts from fawiki, mizan,
irib-news, blogfa, books, persian-tweets-2024 and ganjoor rows, with the
source licences listed above. No development or test sentences supply counts.
orthography-context-names.tsv is licensed under CC BY-SA 4.0 and uses only
training rows from UD Persian Seraji and PerDT (CC BY-SA 4.0).

vowels-lexicon.tsv, vowels-model.json.gz, vowels-sources.json,
vowels-review.tsv and vowels-attribution.jsonl.gz are combined CC BY-SA 4.0
derivatives. They contain word forms, counts, parameters and attribution
metadata, with no definitions, sentences or poem passages. Underlying CC0 and
public-domain contributions retain those terms. The attribution manifest
identifies contributing document IDs, Wikimedia history URLs and revision IDs
where available, and exact input snapshots by filename, byte size and SHA-256.
Shared input files are counted once in that manifest.

Sources: Ganjoor historical verse (local licence unstated), DehkhodaProject
(CC0, https://github.com/DehkhodaProject/DehkhodaProject), Steingass's 1892
dictionary (public domain, https://dsal.uchicago.edu/dictionaries/steingass/;
digital transcription MIT, notice below), English Wiktionary via Kaikki
(https://kaikki.org/dictionary/Persian/), Persian and Tajik Wiktionary, Persian
Wikipedia, Wikisource, Wikibooks, Wikiquote and Wikivoyage (CC BY-SA 4.0),
Persian Wikinews (CC BY 2.5), Common Voice (CC0), HomoRich-G2P-Persian
(CC0, https://huggingface.co/datasets/MahtaFetrat/HomoRich-G2P-Persian;
recommended training split only), Hedayat stories and Mohl's Shahnameh edition
(public-domain works; digital witness licences unstated), non-Sokhan Naskban
and reference OCR editions (printed edition licences unstated), and Mizan
(CC BY 4.0 compilation; underlying translations separately protected).

OCR editions and Mizan contribute extracted lexical facts only. This licence
covers the combined derivative where applicable; it does not relicense the
underlying books, translations or digital witnesses. Their individual source
IDs and licence descriptions remain in vowels-sources.json. Sokhan, Tanzil and
Tashkeela are excluded. Public benchmark text is used only to exclude training
sentence matches and to measure the finished artifacts; no benchmark labels
enter training or calibration. Dictionary headword overlap is expected.

Steingass digital transcription: steingass-scraper, snapshot d2fd36bf577b.
